// REQUIRES: wasmer, wasip1
// XFAIL: *
// RUN: %rene build --manifest-path %S/queue_depth/rene.ncl --build-dir %t.bdir --profile release --target wasm32-wasip1 | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LISTING
// LISTING: queue_depth.wasm
// RUN: %wasmer --stack-size 1048576 %t.bdir/release/queue_depth.wasm | %FileCheck %s
// CHECK: fq = 323752187

// Optimized deep self-recursion should run in constant stack. The package
// churns a Hood-Melville functional queue for 100k rounds through a
// tail-recursive driver whose result (`Pop`, a value enum) returns through
// an sret pointer. On x86-64 and Arm the backends lower the sret self-call
// as a frame-reusing tail call, so the recursion runs in constant stack
// (the native suites cover a million rounds). On wasm32 it stays a real
// `call` — LLVM's WebAssembly lowering does not `return_call` an
// sret-carrying self-call even with the `tail-call` feature enabled — and
// 100k frames exhaust the engine's call stack.
//
// XFAIL until self-recursion of aggregate-returning functions is lowered
// to constant stack on wasm32 too (an MLIR-level rewrite to a loop would
// cover every target at once); the queue itself stays 16 elements long, so
// stack depth is the only variable, and the run flipping to XPASS is the
// signal that the guarantee has landed and this marker should come off.
